Multiprocessor guests

Build the host kernel as well as the monitor:

	cd /sys/src/9/pc64
	mk
	cd /sys/src/cmd/vmx
	mk install

Boot the new kernel before using vmx -P. In the checkout's QEMU environment:

	./boot/qemu -m 4G -smp 4 -kernel sys/src/9/pc64/9pc64

Inside generated9, for example:

	vmx -P 4 -M 512M -c /dev/cons -m initramfs bzImage 'console=ttyS0'

Without -P, the existing single-CPU/PIC configuration remains the default.
With -P 2 or more, each virtual CPU has its own #X instance, registers and
local APIC. All CPUs map the same memory segment. Kernel processes execute
the CPUs concurrently; one monitor event loop serializes exits and device
emulation. Device workers send notifications to that loop. Only the loop
may select the current CPU or use the register cache.

The monitor implements xAPIC physical and logical destinations, fixed and
lowest-priority interrupts, INIT/SIPI, NMIs, local timers, and an I/O APIC
with 24 pins. Intel MP 1.4 tables describe the CPUs and interrupt routes.
Guest CPU count is limited to 64. There is no ACPI, x2APIC, SMI delivery or
APIC address relocation. The 9P debugger continues to select CPU 0.

The host kernel adds INIT and NMI control messages, chooses a supported
EPT page-table memory type, and invalidates the shared VPID after switching
virtual CPUs. Invalidation and guest entry cannot be interrupted by another
context switch. An IPI wakes the processor handling a queued VM command,
instead of waiting for its next clock interrupt. This is necessary to keep
guest timer handlers from consuming all execution time on an SMP host.
All CPUs use the host TSC, including time spent outside the guest. If the
host provides IA32_TSC_ADJUST, a VMCS offset cancels its per-CPU software
adjustments. This preserves a common hardware clock despite the host's
approximate boot-time synchronization. Hardware counters must be synchronized;
without TSC_ADJUST, the host's adjusted counters must be synchronized instead.
The tsc and tscadjust registers implement guest writes to IA32_TSC and
IA32_TSC_ADJUST using per-CPU VMCS offsets. They never write the host clock;
INIT preserves them. The monitor advertises TSC_ADJUST only when the kernel
provides these registers, and invariant TSC only when the host reports it.
CPUID describes one thread per core and one package, and reports the TSC/APIC
frequency ratio at MHz resolution when the host supplies a cycle frequency.
With invariant TSC and TSC_ADJUST, Linux disables its TSC clocksource watchdog,
avoiding comparisons against delayed PIT ticks. Its soft-lockup watchdog remains
enabled; no clocksource or watchdog command-line overrides are needed. The outer
QEMU CPU must expose invariant TSC too; boot/qemu uses host,migratable=off for KVM.

Removing a virtual CPU's ctl, including closing it with ORCLOSE, now destroys
it immediately. Guest memory is released when its last mapping is removed.
Open descriptors hold the CPU object alive, without retaining its mappings;
they cannot reach a new CPU that reuses its directory number. The old practice
of keeping the last removed CPU for debugging is no longer supported; inspect
a stopped CPU before removing its ctl.

Tests

Inside generated9, mk test checks instruction decoding against real APIC
MMIO registers, MSR operand widths, priority/EOI behavior, and timer reloads
with a controlled clock. It runs without VMX hardware. The cases cover 16-
and 32-bit addressing, and on amd64 also REX prefix ordering, RIP-relative,
address-size and segment overrides.

test/run is a Linux-host integration test. It boots generated9 with nested
Intel VMX, builds the native monitor, and runs Linux inside vmx. It requires
the rebuilt generated9 kernel, KVM with nested VMX enabled, a static Linux C
toolchain, and Python 3. A Linux bzImage must have SMP, Intel MP table parsing,
initramfs, devtmpfs, CPU hotplug, Magic SysRq and a serial console enabled.
Virtio PCI and block support must be built in or supplied as matching host
modules. Zstd-compressed modules require Python 3.14.

From the checkout root, with a locally installed Linux kernel:

	sys/src/cmd/vmx/test/run /boot/vmlinuz-VERSION --modules VERSION

If virtio is built in, omit --modules. --cpus selects the test matrix
(default: 1 2 4); --host-cpus selects the outer VM's CPU count. For example,
--host-cpus 2 --cpus 4 exercises more virtual CPUs than host CPUs; with TSC
enabled this currently encounters long stalls and Linux soft lockup reports.
Use at least as many host CPUs as guest CPUs for now.
--timeout sets the limit in seconds per guest (default: 900).

Each guest must report exactly the requested number of online CPUs. Threads
are pinned to separate CPUs and check shared atomic operations, floating
point state, timers, mapping protection changes, isolated forked address
spaces, and concurrent direct virtio disk I/O. The workload is repeated
after two offline/online cycles of each secondary CPU. A SysRq NMI broadcast
and per-CPU interrupt counters check NMI delivery and local APIC timers.
SMP guests must select the TSC clocksource without command-line overrides,
including after CPU hotplug. A five-second guest sleep is compared with the
outer Linux host's clock (four to seven seconds, allowing scheduling delays).
The single-CPU test exercises the original PIC path; it does not require TSC.
That configuration still lacks PIT mode 4, needed for one-shot timer events.

The runner also builds and runs test/6.device on the generated9 host. It checks
TSC reads and writes, positive and negative adjustments, isolation between CPUs,
and preservation across INIT. Guest RDTSC must agree with the register interface.
The lifetime checks cover
32 allocation/removal cycles with 64 MiB mappings, ORCLOSE, explicit quit and
remove, failed opens, concurrent register reads, blocked waits, and stale open
descriptors and bound directories after slot reuse. It verifies that guest
pages are released even while old descriptors remain open. To run it alone:

	mk test/6.device
	test/6.device

In an amd64 generated9 guest, a native process test is also available:

	cd /sys/src/cmd/vmx
	mk test/6.plan9
	test/6.plan9 2

The argument is the expected number of CPUs. The test counts /dev/sysstat
entries, pins one process to each CPU, and checks shared atomic increments,
floating-point state across yields, and the monotonic clock.

The Linux test creates its own disposable disks. Logs and generated images remain
under usr/glenda/tmp/vmx-smp-* and are not source files. It does not download
or vendor Linux or its modules. Check the complete guest and monitor logs
when investigating a failure; recognizing multiple CPUs alone is not a pass.

Validated on an amd64 generated9 host under Intel nested KVM:

	Linux 6.18.56: 1, 2 and 4 CPUs on 4 host CPUs.
	generated9: boot to rc and the native process test, with 2 and 4 CPUs
	            on 2 host CPUs.

The limit of 64 CPUs is not a claim of testing all sizes. The 386 sources
compile with 8c; runtime tests above used amd64.

Architecture references

Intel 64 and IA-32 Architectures Software Developer's Manual:
Volume 2, section 2.2.1: REX prefixes and instruction encoding.
Volume 2, WRMSR entry: MSR operand widths.
Volume 3: processor initialization, local APICs, VMX event injection, EPT and VPID.
Volume 3B, section 19.17.3: IA32_TSC_ADJUST and per-CPU TSC writes.
https://www.intel.com/content/www/us/en/developer/articles/technical/intel-sdm.html

Linux 6.18, arch/x86/kernel/tsc.c: check_system_tsc_reliable and
calibrate_delay_is_known describe clock verification and delay calibration.
https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.18/arch/x86/kernel/tsc.c

Intel MultiProcessor Specification, version 1.4: configuration tables and
interrupt assignments. The implementation is new generated9 code; the
existing vmx code retains its upstream authorship and license.
